CASES = [
 ('1', 'The left side of the plate', 'A 74-year-old woman one month after a stroke keeps catching her left shoulder on door frames and leaves the food on the left side of her plate. She doesn\'t notice either. At intake she can\'t recall her discharge date, and her son fills in most of her history.',
  ['Which two impairments do you suspect?', 'How would you test them?'],
  ['<b>Left visual neglect</b>: doesn\'t attend to the left (bumps door frames, leaves food on the left of the plate). Screen with <b>line bisection</b> and <b>star cancellation</b>; also test confrontation <b>visual fields</b> to rule out a field cut.', '<b>Cognitive impairment</b> (memory): poor recall of events. Screen with the <b>MoCA</b> and document as a positive or negative screen, not a diagnosis.', 'Interview tip: sit in her line of vision, empower her first, then ask permission to involve her son.'],
  ['Neglect', 'MoCA'], '{{u_exam,ting11,krish17,u_subj}}'),
 ('3', 'Drowsy on day 2', 'On day 2 after a large stroke, the patient opens his eyes only after you call loudly and gently shake his shoulder. He answers slowly and needs repeated stimulation to keep responding.',
  ['What level of consciousness is this?', 'How does it shape your exam?'],
  ['<b>Obtunded</b>: arousable but difficult, needs repeated stimulation, slowed responses. (Lethargic patients can be roused with stimulation and show awareness, but fall back asleep easily.)', 'Choose exam activities suited to that level: short, simple, repeated arousal; defer tests that need sustained attention such as the MoCA. <span class="mine">inference</span>'],
  ['Levels of consciousness'], '{{tindall90,fell18}}'),
 ('3', 'A tongue that drifts', 'On CN XII testing, your patient\'s tongue deviates to the right on protrusion. There are fasciculations on the right side of the tongue.',
  ['Where is the lesion more likely?', 'How do you document it?'],
  ['With a CN XII (peripheral) lesion the tongue deviates <b>toward</b> the lesion; with a cortical or corticobulbar lesion it deviates <b>away</b>. Deviation to the right fits a <b>right CN XII</b> lesion. Fasciculations are one of the involuntary movements you are asked to look for.', 'Document: tongue deviates R on protrusion; fasciculations present; strength normal, weak or absent by side; note any dysarthria.'],
  ['CN XII'], '{{u_cn}}'),
 ('3', 'Dizzy after a virus', 'A 45-year-old reports dizziness. On the head impulse test, when you quickly turn her head to the left, her eyes drift with the head and then make a corrective saccade back to your nose. Her static acuity is 20/20; with 2 Hz head oscillation she reads 20/50.',
  ['Interpret the HIT.', 'Interpret the DVA.'],
  ['Corrective saccade after a <b>left</b> head turn → likely <b>left</b> peripheral vestibular loss: document "+L head impulse test".', '20/20 → 20/50 is a drop of more than 3 lines → <b>VOR impairment</b>. Document static and dynamic acuity, position, and plane (yaw).'],
  ['HIT', 'DVA'], '{{u_cn}}'),
 ('4', 'Catch at the elbow', 'Moving the patient\'s left elbow quickly into extension, you feel a catch followed by mild resistance through the rest of the range, less than half of it. Slowly, the arm moves freely.',
  ['Spasticity or rigidity?', 'MAS grade and documentation?'],
  ['Velocity-dependent catch → <b>spasticity</b>.', 'Catch followed by minimal resistance through less than half the range → <b>MAS 1+</b>.', 'Document by muscle group: "MAS 1+ L elbow flexors". Test the less-affected side first for a baseline.'],
  ['MAS'], '{{bohan87,u_exam}}'),
 ('4', 'Strong hip, weak ankle', 'On the UMCT, your patient flexes the hip past 60° three times in 8 seconds, flexes the knee to 45° three times in 9 seconds, and can\'t dorsiflex to a right angle. In single-limb stance with the knees at 30°, the knee keeps collapsing.',
  ['Grade each part you have data for.', 'Which gait phases are affected?'],
  ['Hip flexion <b>Strong</b> (&gt; 60° ×3 in 10 s); knee flexion <b>Moderate</b> (30–60° ×3 in 10 s); ankle DF <b>Weak</b> (no Moderate grade exists for DF); knee extension <b>Weak</b> (continues to collapse).', 'Flexion tests reflect the <b>swing</b> limb; extension tests reflect <b>stance</b>. Expect limited foot clearance in swing and poor knee control in stance. <span class="mine">inference</span>'],
  ['UMCT'], '{{hislop95}}'),
 ('5', 'Words that come out wrong', 'A 68-year-old after a left stroke talks easily and at length, but you can\'t follow the meaning. He calls his wife "my table" and uses made-up words. He doesn\'t seem to notice his errors.',
  ['Fluent or non-fluent?', 'Name two error types and two communication strategies.'],
  ['<b>Fluent aphasia</b>: effortless, long sentences, empty content, decreased insight.', 'Errors: <b>semantic paraphasia</b> ("table" for wife) and <b>neologisms</b> (made-up words).', 'Strategies: short, simple, direct language; verify understanding ("I heard you say ___, is that right?"); use gestures, writing or pictures; don\'t pretend to understand.'],
  ['Aphasia'], '{{u_slp}}'),
 ('5', 'The wet voice at lunch', 'During a session over lunch, your patient with PD coughs after sips of water, and his voice sounds wet afterward. His wife says he had pneumonia twice this year.',
  ['What are you concerned about?', 'What do you do?'],
  ['<b>Dysphagia with aspiration</b>: coughing, wet voice after swallowing and recurrent pulmonary infections are warning signs. About half of people with dysphagia aspirate silently, so the absence of coughing wouldn\'t reassure you either.', '<b>Refer to SLP</b> (known PD, signs of aspiration). Instrumental testing (MBSS or FEES) is their call. Note risk factors such as oral care dependence and limited ambulation.'],
  ['Dysphagia', 'Referral'], '{{u_slp,panebi20}}'),
 ('6', 'HIIT after stroke', 'A 62-year-old 8 months after stroke walks safely on a treadmill with a harness. You want to start high-intensity interval training.',
  ['Give a dose using FITT.', 'What do you monitor?'],
  ['<b>F:</b> 2–5 days/week for 2–4 weeks. <b>I:</b> fastest safe walking speed or 85–95% HRR. <b>T:</b> 25–30 min sessions; bursts such as 30 s with 30–60 s recovery, up to 3 min on/4 min off. <b>T:</b> treadmill if safe.', 'Monitor intensity and vital signs; if you use a recumbent stepper, watch for hypotensive responses.'],
  ['HIIT'], '{{crozier18}}'),
 ('6', 'Tired by set two', 'A patient with MS says she stopped exercising because she\'s exhausted by the second set and her legs feel worse in the heat.',
  ['How do you adjust the program?'],
  ['Keep the challenge and change the dose: <b>shorten bouts</b> (fewer reps or less time, same task), <b>plan rest</b> before fatigue overwhelms performance and use it to return to the task, and <b>let symptoms guide progression</b>.', 'Combine moderate strength training (≥ 2×/week) with aerobic work; aerobic exercise is beneficial without causing exacerbations. Account for heat sensitivity and recovery time.'],
  ['MS dosing'], '{{u_ther,halab17,tallner12}}'),
 ('7', 'Rolling to the weak side', 'A 72-year-old woman, 4 months after a right stroke, rolls to the left independently but needs min assist and a verbal cue to bring her left arm across when rolling right. Her left arm moves in a flexion synergy when she reaches.',
  ['Why is rolling right harder?', 'Give two facilitation ideas.'],
  ['Rolling right means the involved <b>left</b> UE and LE must lead across midline; post-stroke, rolling toward the non-affected side tends to rely on pulling with the non-paretic arm while the paretic side follows passively.', 'Ideas: "where the eyes, head and neck go, the trunk will follow"; PNF D1 flexion ("follow your hand with your eyes… squeeze, turn and pull up and across"); LE D1 flexion ("toes up, heel up and across"); replication from the end position; reverse chop with manual contacts on the pelvis.'],
  ['Rolling', 'PNF'], '{{zukow25,hooge09,u_roll,u_ue2}}'),
 ('7', 'Dropping into the chair', 'The same patient stands with contact guard, pushing up with her right arm and leg. When she sits down, she drops into the chair with poor control.',
  ['Which phase and contraction type is the problem?', 'Pick a PNF technique to train it.'],
  ['Stand to sit requires <b>controlled eccentric</b> forces throughout, especially through the momentum-transfer phase until the buttocks land.', '<b>Combination of isotonics</b> (concentric, hold, eccentric without relaxation) or part-task practice of the lowering phase. Also consider forced use or error augmentation to load the left LE.'],
  ['Stand to sit'], '{{u_sts,u_ther}}'),
 ('8', 'Four days post-stroke, flaccid arm', 'A 61-year-old man is 4 days after a right stroke. His left UE is hypotonic with no active movement, and you can palpate an inferior shoulder subluxation. In sitting, the left arm hangs at his side and he props on his right hand.',
  ['List three priorities for the left UE.', 'Would you use a sling? FES?'],
  ['Priorities: <b>positioning and handling</b> to protect the shoulder (supported arm in bed and chair); <b>early mobility</b> and weight bearing through the left UE with facilitation; start work on the <b>hand</b> to drive proximal demand and prevent learned non-use.', 'Slings: evidence is insufficient and they are likely not beneficial. FES over supraspinatus and posterior deltoid (10–50 Hz, 2.5 s on/off, 3–5 days/week ≥ 4 weeks) can reduce subluxation (~4.9 mm on average).'],
  ['Hypotonia', 'Subluxation'], '{{u_ue1,u_ue2,ada05,lavi22,vafadar15}}'),
 ('8', 'Should we strengthen a spastic arm?', 'A family member worries that strengthening exercises will make their father\'s spastic arm "tighter".',
  ['What does the evidence say?', 'What is the goal of treatment?'],
  ['Resistance training improves strength, gait speed, function and quality of life <b>without exacerbating spasticity</b>, and may benefit spasticity itself. Weakness affects function more than spasticity does.', 'Goals are <b>functional and patient-centered</b>, never "decrease spasticity". Strengthen out of pattern where possible; use inhibition techniques (deep tendon pressure, rhythmic rotation, sustained stretch) as temporary preparation.'],
  ['Hypertonia'], '{{pak08,chacon24,ross07,u_ue2}}'),
 ('9', 'Writing the O and A', 'Today you did forced-use weight shifting onto the left LE in standing (5 min, min A), then sit-to-stand × 10 with VC for foot placement 3/5 reps.',
  ['Write one O line and one A line.'],
  ['<span class="mine">example</span> O: "Neuromuscular re-ed: forced-use weight shift to L LE in standing at counter × 5 min, min A at pelvis, to improve paretic-limb weight acceptance for gait and transfers. STS × 10 from 18\" mat, VC for L foot placement 3/5 reps, CGA."', '<span class="mine">example</span> A: "Pt demonstrates improved L LE weight acceptance with reduced reliance on R UE push-off, reducing fall risk during transfers; continues to require VC for foot placement. Would benefit from continued skilled PT."'],
  ['SOAP'], '{{u_soap}}'),
]

